<div class="section" id="key-takeaways">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-number">06</span>
    <span class="section-title">Key Takeaways &amp; Strategic Implications</span>
  </div>
  <p class="section-desc">
    Data-grounded conclusions for investors, platform strategists, and policymakers. Each finding is anchored to
    specific metrics from this dataset.
  </p>

  <div class="card">
    <ul class="takeaway-list">
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">01</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Amsterdam is the clear premium pricing market among cities with price data</h4>
          <p>
            With a median nightly price of $222, Amsterdam sits 65% above London ($135) and 69% above Rome ($131).
            Its tight IQR ($160–$315) suggests relatively consistent premium positioning with fewer budget-tier listings.
            For investors targeting yield per listing rather than volume, Amsterdam's pricing power is unmatched in
            this dataset — though its small listing count (1,770) limits absolute market size.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Amsterdam median: $222</span>
          <span class="data-point">London median: $135</span>
          <span class="data-point">Rome median: $131</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">02</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Bangkok and Barcelona are heavily commercialized — professional hosts control 58–60% of supply</h4>
          <p>
            Bangkok (60.4%) and Barcelona (58.3%) lead the dataset in professional host share, meaning most supply
            is controlled by multi-property operators. In Barcelona, individual hosts have a mean portfolio of 63.4
            listings and one host holds 514. This concentration creates competitive barriers for new individual hosts
            and signals a business-operator-dominated market. Regulators in both cities face enforcement challenges
            given this entrenched commercialization.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Bangkok pro-host: 60.4%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Barcelona pro-host: 58.3%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Barcelona max portfolio: 514</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">03</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Amsterdam's regulations demonstrably suppress professional hosting — the most regulated market in the dataset</h4>
          <p>
            At just 6.8% professional host share and a median availability of only 18 days per year, Amsterdam's
            short-term rental restrictions (annual night caps, single-listing rules) are producing measurable market
            effects. For policymakers, Amsterdam offers the clearest case study of regulatory effectiveness. For
            investors, it signals a high-barrier-to-entry market with constrained supply — potentially supportive of
            premium pricing.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Amsterdam pro-host: 6.8%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Median availability: 18 days</span>
          <span class="data-point">Max host portfolio: 35 listings</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">04</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Rome leads on guest engagement despite mid-range pricing — the most active market by review velocity</h4>
          <p>
            Rome averages 1.77 reviews per month per listing — 79% above Bangkok (0.96) and 27% above the 7-city
            average. With 66.3% of listings receiving a review in the last 12 months, Rome demonstrates consistently
            high booking activity. Combined with a median price of $131 (competitive among European markets), Rome
            presents a high-demand, accessible pricing combination that supports strong occupancy for STR operators.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Rome reviews/month: 1.77</span>
          <span class="data-point">Recent review rate: 66.3%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Rome median price: $131</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">05</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Barcelona shows strong regulatory signals: 40% of listings require 30+ night minimum stays</h4>
          <p>
            Barcelona's 40.1% long-stay rate — far above any other city — indicates that a significant share of hosts
            have repositioned to medium-term rentals to circumvent STR licensing requirements or respond to bans.
            This bifurcated market (true short-term vs. displaced medium-term inventory) complicates competitive
            analysis and suggests that Barcelona's effective STR supply is materially smaller than raw listing counts imply.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">≥30 night min: 40.1%</span>
          <span class="data-point">3rd highest pro-host share</span>
          <span class="data-point">Mean avail: 193.9 days</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">06</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Paris and Sydney pricing data gaps limit cross-market analysis — a critical data quality issue</h4>
          <p>
            Paris (13,927 listings, 27.9% of the dataset) and Sydney (3,013 listings) have zero usable price records —
            a significant blind spot for any pricing-driven investment thesis involving these markets. Non-pricing
            metrics confirm Paris as a high-entire-home-share market (88.2%) with moderate professional host activity
            (21.4%), while Sydney shows strong guest engagement (1.44 reviews/month). Any pricing conclusions about
            these cities require sourcing from supplementary data.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Paris price records: 0</span>
          <span class="data-point">Sydney price records: 0</span>
          <span class="data-point">Paris entire home share: 88.2%</span>
        </div>
      </li>
      <li>
        <div class="takeaway-num">07</div>
        <div class="takeaway-content">
          <h4>Paris dominates supply with 88% entire-home listings — the most commoditized entire-unit market</h4>
          <p>
            Paris has the highest entire-home share in the dataset (88.2%), followed by Amsterdam (82.8%) and
            Sydney (79.0%). Markets with very high entire-home shares leave little room for private-room budget
            alternatives and indicate a strong commercial orientation. For platform strategy, these cities represent
            the deepest pool of traditional "hotel substitute" inventory — relevant for pricing benchmarking against
            traditional accommodation.
          </p>
          <span class="data-point">Paris entire home: 88.2%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Amsterdam: 82.8%</span>
          <span class="data-point">Sydney: 79.0%</span>
